**Theorem 79.3 (original entry and actual advanced current have different criteria).** Under precisely the fixed-geometry acquisition policy of Definition 79.1:

1. Uniform exact original-entry reporting is possible if and only if $g=1$.
2. Uniform exact reporting of the whole actual final current field, or installation of a self-contained predictor of its full original future service, is possible if and only if $g\le2$.

A self-contained predictor here predicts from its installed state and future commands; it cannot defer missing numerical information to another dependent archive or an additional source assay. The positive protocols are given in §80. At $g=2$, entry information is lost but exact current acquisition is possible because the only hidden mode is a zero mode. At $g\ge3$ the current obstruction survives every finite allowed acquisition schedule.

**Proof of necessity.** If $g>1$, (79.3) supplies a nonzero rational $\eta\in\mathcal W$. On paired entries $x,x+\eta$, common fixed-graph Adds cancel and after $t$ Ticks the field difference is $A^t\eta$. Every permitted Read annihilates it. Freeze a terminating adaptive path at one entry. Induction on complete replies makes the other entry follow the same choices, operands, frames and stop. A common original report cannot equal two different entries. This proves the first necessity, independently of whether the hidden difference is eventually erased.

If $g\ge3$, then $g-1-\epsilon_g>0$. Choose instead a nonzero rational $\eta\in\mathcal W\cap\operatorname{im}A$. The same paired-path proof applies, but $A^t\eta\ne0$ at every finite $t$, because $A$ is injective on its image. Thus the two actual final fields remain different on the same graph. A common full current report cannot be correct on both. For the predictor task, (75.1) supplies a finite legal future all-leaf continuation with different scalar replies on this difference. Both omitted ports remain fully available after handoff. Identical self-contained predictors could not predict both complete continuations. This proves the second necessity. Theorem 80.1 proves the converses. $\square$

These are obstructions for this observation policy, fixed geometry and the complete owed continuation. They do not apply if growth is used during acquisition, an omitted leaf is Read, Reset is installed, dependent advice is supplied, or the future task is permanently reduced to the same restricted observations. They imply no physical or universal impossibility.

